  "_note": "Bluegill (Lepomis macrochirus), a common sunfish of Midwest lakes. Markings from field-guide descriptions: a deep, round, flat body; olive back; 6 to 8 dark vertical bars on the sides; an orange-yellow breast; a solid black ear flap at the back of the gill cover; a dark spot at the rear of the soft dorsal fin; a small mouth. From above, as seen from a boat, the shape is exaggerated: short and stubby, with round pectoral fins splayed out. The dorsal and anal fins are solid wedges and the tail a lens, so they show from above too. 170 mm long, the base length (SECOND-ORDER R1.150-R1.169).",
